  "sections": [
    {
      "sectionId": "section-introduction",
      "heading": "Introduction",
      "sourceAnchor": "introduction",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Introduced in Guildmaster’s Guide to Ravnica, the Simic Hybrid is a novel\n\trace that hasn’t been reused in other campaign settings. Created by one of\n\tRavnica’s guilds, the Simic Hybrid is a human magically altered with animalian\n\tfeatures to make them more physically capable. But these aren’t just animal\n\tpeople like a crab person or something like that; instead, picture a human\n\twith animal parts grafted onto their bodies like giant crab claws growing from\n\tan extra set of limbs."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Mechanically, the Simic Hybrid is really neat. They’re the only published\n\trace with decision points past first level, gaining additional enhancements as\n\tyour level increases and thereby gaining new racial traits. Their ability\n\tscore increases are perfect for any class, they get Darkvision, and the Animal\n\tEnhancement options are diverse enough that most classes can find some helpful\n\toptions."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-3",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The introduction of the custom origin rules in Tasha’s Cauldron of Everything\n\tdid very little to change the Simic Hybrid. The only difference is that you\n\tcan now put that +2 Constitution increase somewhere else. In most cases, that\n\tjust means switching the +1 and the +2 increases within the same build, such\n\tas taking +2 Intelligence and +1 Constitution for a wizard, but it does open\n\tup options for MAD classes like the Monk and the Paladin to put those\n\tincreases elsewhere."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
